    "narrative": "The Zurich Zebras enter the September 3rd slate with **Yordan Alvarez** pacing the lineup at a 75.57 score. He remains our cornerstone play today against Castillo, L, as his elite .451 xwOBA and favorable #2 order slot outweigh the neutral-to-negative park effects. **Junior Caminero** also projects strongly at 70.79, benefiting significantly from a premium #3 spot in the order and a favorable platoon matchup against Quantrill. Both players offer the stability required to anchor our offensive output today.\n\nWhile **Josh Smith** narrowly missed the 60-point \"Strong\" threshold with a 59.71, he remains a viable pivot given the high-upside potential for stolen bases against Bibee. We are prioritizing speed across the board, which is why **Pete Crow-Armstrong** (63.14) is a locked-in start; despite facing a tougher arm in Henderson (3.08 xERA), his elite sprint speed and #1 lineup position provide the necessary catalyst for our SB-starved Roto campaign. **Caleb Durbin** also remains in the lineup (68.74) because his ability to exploit the high delivery time of Young should translate into meaningful base-path opportunities, offsetting any risk from the SP's skill level.\n\nRegarding bench decisions, **Chase Meidroth** (44.40) is our most marginal start, hanging onto the lineup by a thread due to a lack of deeper alternatives in today's rotation. With **Trea Turner**, **Kazuma Okamoto**, **Colt Keith**, and **Carson Benge** all inactive or off-schedule, we are forced to weather the dip in production from the bottom of the order. We continue to monitor the status of **Gary Sanchez**, **Lars Nootbaar**, **Casey Schmitt**, **Dillon Dingler**, **Jacob Gonzalez**, **Hector Rodriguez**, **Luis Pena**, and **Pedro Ramirez** for potential late-game pinch-hit opportunities if the scores tighten in the later innings. Risks are limited today, though **Christian Yelich** faces a stiff test against Gausman, relying heavily on a positive platoon advantage to maintain his 62.61 efficiency rating.",
